duction and again if  you put that together  with the protection that you're getting  against infection in the first place  that means that there's almost 85  percent  protection against dying with a single  dose of the vaccine  and again we see better figures after  the second dose  uh on top of that uh our data in  healthcare workers has shown that  healthcare workers who are vaccinated  have a 70 reduction in their risk of  getting infected  after a single dose and that's whether  they are a symptomatic case  or an asymptomatic case and that means  they are therefore  less likely to spread to vulnerable  individuals that they care for  and the really exciting data that came  from scotland last week  suggested that those vaccinated  healthcare workers have a 30  chance lower chance of passing infection  onto their  household contacts as secretive states  already mentioned  so this is really the first evidence we  have of a reduction in transmission  from vaccinating and it means that the  more people we vaccinate  the more we are going to be able to  reduce the spread of infection  so uh overall this is really now  translating into seeing a decline  in deaths and serious cases from this  infection  and it means every day we vaccinate more  people we are preventing more deaths  so uh i think we also  know as i think we've seen the antibody  data we've done a study today again  which shows that 75 percent of that  older group have protection and  immunity in their blood so again very  good  results so overall we will expect to see  as more people are vaccinated  a bigger impact on cases on  hospitalizations  and on deaths so i just use this  opportunity to encourage anybody who's  eligible for vaccine to come forward to  take the vaccine  both vaccines work incredibly  effectively i myself had astrazeneca  vaccine on friday  and i'm very pleased that i'm now  hopefully as well protected as the  people we've been studying  thank you thank you very much dr ramsey  pro