  aubry uh alegretti at the guardian  good afternoon mr hancock uh scientists  doctors the bereaved and now dominick  cummings have urged for a lessons  learned public inquiry  into the government's handling of  coronavirus why can't we have one now  are you concerned that the department of  health couldn't cope with the pressure  of public inquiry at the same time as  the pandemic  and to professor van tan has every  lesson that could have been learned so  far  been learned if not then wouldn't a  public inquiry ensure that happens  sooner rather than later well we're less  we're learning lessons all the time i  mean the  we're learning lessons from the vaccine  rollout you've just heard some of the  extra evidence uh we constantly look at  uh the actions that we take and work out  how we can  how we can make sure the response is as  good as possible  so that is happening all of the time  professor bantam  yes thank you for the question i mean i  think the timing of inquiries  is entirely a matter for ministers and  politicians it's not a matter  for physicians  personally would the an inquiry be  an unwelcome distraction for me  personally  at the moment when i'm very focused on  the vaccine  program and the vaccine program we might  need  in the autumn who knows i think it would  be  an extra burden that wasn't necessary  is looking back on what you did and  whether you could do  things better a function of medicine  in terms of the clinical audit that  happens in every  gp surgery and every nhs trust up and  down the land  yes it is will lessons be learned in due  course i am sure lessons will emerge  uh thanks very much uh final question is  from emily ashton from bloomberg emily  hello and for matt hancock i just wanted  to ask you say that the vaccination  targets will still be met  is there any scope then to bring forward  the dates of the roadmap  freezing lockdown or are they absolutely  the earliest dates possible  and for mr hancock and professor van tam